System.out.println(c.equals(d));

Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference? Predict the output of this code.

class Box { int value; }

static void change(Box b, int n) {
  b.value = 50;
  b = new Box();
  b.value = 99;
  n = 99;
}

// in main
Box box = new Box();
int num = 10;
change(box, num);
System.out.println(box.value + " " + num);

model_answer:
import java.util.List;

abstract class Shape {
  abstract double area();
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  private final double r;
  Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
  @Override double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
  private final double w, h;
  Rectangle(double w, double h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }
  @Override double area() { return w * h; }
}

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    List<Shape> shapes = List.of(new Circle(1), new Rectangle(2, 3));
    for (Shape s : shapes) {
      System.out.printf("%s: %.2f%n", s.getClass().getSimpleName(), s.area());
    }
  }
}

It prints Circle: 3.14 and Rectangle: 6.00. The subclasses override area() with the same signature, and although the loop variable is a superclass reference of type Shape, the JVM calls the version of the actual object at runtime (dynamic dispatch, i.e. runtime polymorphism). @Override makes the compiler verify that a method is really being overridden. Shape is an abstract class here, but a concrete base method returning 0 would work the same way.

