// in main
Parent p = new Child();
System.out.println(p.name);
System.out.println(p.type());
System.out.println(p.show());

model_answer:
I would add sendBulk as a default method in the interface, implemented on top of the existing abstract method:

default void sendBulk(List<String> recipients, String message) {
  for (String r : recipients) {
    send(r, message);
  }
}

All existing implementations keep compiling and working because they inherit the default, which preserves backward compatibility. Classes that can do better, such as an email notifier with a batch API, simply override it. This kind of interface evolution is exactly why Java 8 introduced default methods (for example Collection.stream() and Iterable.forEach()). I would keep the default method stateless, document it, and remember that if a class implements two interfaces that both define the same default method, it must override it to resolve the conflict.

public interface PaymentMethod {
  PaymentResult pay(Order order);
  PaymentType type();
}
public record PaymentResult(String transactionId, PaymentStatus status, String message) {}
public enum PaymentStatus { SUCCESS, FAILED, PENDING }

Each method is its own class implementing the interface: UpiPayment, CardPayment and WalletPayment, each with its own data and validation (VPA, card token, wallet balance). Steps they all share (validate, call the gateway, record the transaction) go in an abstract class AbstractPayment using the template method pattern, with subclasses filling in only the specific steps. CheckoutService depends only on the PaymentMethod interface and gets the right implementation from a factory or a Map<PaymentType, PaymentMethod> registry, so it calls pay() through polymorphism without any if-else on the type; this is the Strategy pattern. Adding NetBankingPayment means writing one new class and registering it, and checkout code does not change (Open/Closed Principle). Failures are reported with a custom exception such as PaymentException or a FAILED result. Amounts use BigDecimal, never double, and only tokenised card data is stored, never the raw card number.

question:
A developer overrides equals() in Employee so that two employees with the same id are equal, but a HashSet still stores duplicates. Why, and how do you fix it?

class Employee {
  private final int id;
  private final String name;
  Employee(int id, String name) { this.id = id; this.name = name; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (!(o instanceof Employee e)) return false;
    return id == e.id;
  }
}

Set<Employee> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(new Employee(1, "Asha"));
set.add(new Employee(1, "Asha"));
System.out.println(set.size()); // prints 2, expected 1

model_answer:
Only equals() was overridden, not hashCode(), so each object still uses Object's default identity hash code. A HashSet is backed by a HashMap: it uses hashCode() to choose a bucket and calls equals() only for entries in that bucket whose hash codes match. The two equal employees have different identity hash codes, so equals() is never even called and both are stored. This breaks the equals contract: objects that are equal must have equal hash codes. Fix it by overriding hashCode() with the same fields used in equals():

@Override public int hashCode() {
  return Integer.hashCode(id); // or Objects.hash(id)
}

Alternatively model it as a record, which generates consistent equals() and hashCode(). The fields used in hashCode() should also not change while the object is inside a HashSet or used as a HashMap key.

