model_answer:
public class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {
  private final BigDecimal shortfall;

  public InsufficientFundsException(String message, BigDecimal shortfall) {
    super(message);
    this.shortfall = shortfall;
  }
  public BigDecimal getShortfall() { return shortfall; }
}

// in BankAccount (balance is a BigDecimal field)
public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {
  if (amount.signum() <= 0) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
  }
  if (amount.compareTo(balance) > 0) {
    throw new InsufficientFundsException("Insufficient balance", amount.subtract(balance));
  }
  balance = balance.subtract(amount);
}

// caller
try {
  account.withdraw(new BigDecimal("5000"));
} catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
  System.out.println(e.getMessage() + ", short by " + e.getShortfall());
}

Extending Exception makes it a checked exception, so the throws clause is required and callers must handle or declare it, which suits an expected, recoverable business condition. The constructor passes the message to super(message) so getMessage() works, and the extra shortfall field gives callers useful data. Invalid input is a programming error, so it uses the unchecked IllegalArgumentException; extend RuntimeException instead if you do not want to force handling.

The close() calls sit at the end of the try block, so whenever an exception is thrown before them, on the exception path, the resources are never closed. This resource leak adds up: every failed request leaves a file handle and a connection open until the operating system limit is hit and the connection pool runs dry. The fix is try-with-resources, which closes every resource declared in its header automatically, in reverse order, whether the block completes normally or throws:

try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection();
     BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) {
  // build the report
}

Any class that implements AutoCloseable can be used this way. If both the body and close() throw, the body's exception is propagated and the close failure is attached as a suppressed exception (getSuppressed()). After the fix I would watch the pool's active-connection metric to confirm the leak is gone rather than just raising limits.

