String role = scanner.nextLine();
if (role == "ADMIN") {
  grantAdminAccess();
}

model_answer:
public static String reverse(String s) {
  if (s == null) return null;
  char[] chars = s.toCharArray();
  int left = 0, right = chars.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    char tmp = chars[left];
    chars[left++] = chars[right];
    chars[right--] = tmp;
  }
  return new String(chars);
}

The method copies the string into a char array and uses two pointers to swap characters from both ends until they meet, so it runs in O(n) time. A copy is needed because String is immutable, and new String(chars) builds the result. It handles null, empty and one-character input. In production code new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString() is simpler and also keeps surrogate pairs such as emoji together, which this char-based version does not. Building the result with += in a loop should be avoided because every concatenation creates a new String.

public static Map<String, Integer> wordFrequency(String text) {
  Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
  for (String word : text.toLowerCase().split("[^a-z0-9]+")) {
    if (!word.isEmpty()) {
      counts.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum);
    }
  }
  return counts;
}

The text is converted with toLowerCase() and then split on a regex of one or more non-alphanumeric characters, which removes spaces and punctuation; a leading punctuation mark can produce one empty token, so empty strings are skipped. merge(word, 1, Integer::sum) stores 1 for a new word or adds 1 to the existing count; the older equivalent is counts.put(word, counts.getOrDefault(word, 0) + 1). The whole method is O(n) in the length of the text. HashMap does not guarantee any order, so use a LinkedHashMap for first-seen order or a TreeMap for alphabetical output.

question:
This code throws ConcurrentModificationException. Why, and how do you fix it?

List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Asha", "Ravi", "Amit", "Neha"));
for (String name : names) {
  if (name.startsWith("A")) {
    names.remove(name);
  }
}

Two threads each call increment() 10,000 times on the same Counter object, but the final count is often less than 20,000. Explain the bug and fix it.

class Counter {
  private int count = 0;
  public void increment() { count++; }
  public int get() { return count; }
}

model_answer:
count++ is not atomic: it is a read-modify-write sequence (read count, add 1, write it back). When both threads read the same value before either writes, both write value + 1 and one update is lost. This is a race condition on shared mutable state. Fixes:

// Option 1: intrinsic lock
public synchronized void increment() { count++; }
public synchronized int get() { return count; }

// Option 2: lock-free atomic
private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();
public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }
public int get() { return count.get(); }

synchronized gives mutual exclusion and visibility, so only one thread updates at a time and readers see the latest value. AtomicInteger uses compare-and-swap (CAS) without locking and is usually the better choice for a simple counter; LongAdder scales better under very high contention. Marking the field volatile alone does not fix it: volatile guarantees visibility but not atomicity of count++. Also call join() on both threads before reading the result.

question:
What does this code print, and why?

List<Integer> nums = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6);
Stream<Integer> s = nums.stream()
    .filter(n -> { System.out.println("filter " + n); return n % 2 == 0; })
    .map(n -> { System.out.println("map " + n); return n * 10; });
System.out.println("pipeline built");
System.out.println(s.findFirst().get());

Refactor this method with Optional so that it has no explicit null checks and returns "UNKNOWN" when any value is missing. Assume the repository now offers `Optional<User> findUser(long id)`.

String getCity(long id) {
  User user = repo.findUserOrNull(id);
  if (user != null && user.getAddress() != null
      && user.getAddress().getCity() != null) {
    return user.getAddress().getCity().toUpperCase();
  }
  return "UNKNOWN";
}

model_answer:
String getCity(long id) {
  return repo.findUser(id)          // Optional<User>
      .map(User::getAddress)        // empty if the address is null
      .map(Address::getCity)
      .map(String::toUpperCase)
      .orElse("UNKNOWN");
}

map() applies the method reference only when a value is present and turns a null result into an empty Optional, so any missing link short-circuits straight to orElse(). Avoid calling get() without a check, because it throws NoSuchElementException on an empty Optional; orElseThrow() states that intent explicitly. Use orElseGet(() -> computeDefault()) when the default is expensive, since the argument of orElse is always evaluated. Optional is designed as a method return type; it should not be used for fields, method parameters or collections (return an empty list instead).

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public static void extractErrors(Path in, Path out) throws IOException {
  try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(in);
       BufferedWriter writer = Files.newBufferedWriter(out)) {
    String line;
    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
      if (line.contains("ERROR")) {
        writer.write(line);
        writer.newLine();
      }
    }
  }
}

Files.newBufferedReader and Files.newBufferedWriter work with a Path, use UTF-8 by default and are buffered, so the file is processed one line at a time without loading it all into memory; readLine() returns null at the end of the file. try-with-resources closes both the reader and the writer even if an exception occurs. IOException is a checked exception, so it is declared with throws (or handled by the caller). By default newBufferedWriter creates the output file or truncates an existing one; pass StandardOpenOption.APPEND to append instead.

readAllLines loads every line into a List<String>, so a 6 GB file (even larger as Java strings) cannot fit in the heap. The fix is streaming: process one line at a time and keep only the aggregate, using a BufferedReader loop or Files.lines() inside try-with-resources, because the stream holds an open file handle:

try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
  Map<String, Long> countByLevel = lines
      .map(line -> line.split(" ", 3))
      .filter(parts -> parts.length >= 2)
      .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(parts -> parts[1], Collectors.counting()));
}

This assumes lines like '2026-09-30T10:15:00 ERROR message', and memory use now stays roughly constant whatever the file size. I would also skip or log malformed lines instead of failing the whole job, set the charset explicitly, avoid collecting per-line objects into lists, log progress, and consider parallel processing only if the work is CPU-heavy, since disk reading is usually the bottleneck. Increasing -Xmx is not a real fix; it only delays the failure as the logs grow.

Spring resolves constructor parameters by type (injection by type). There are two beans of type PaymentGateway in the context, so Spring cannot choose one and startup fails with a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException. Fixes:
1) Mark the default implementation with @Primary so it is injected whenever no specific bean is requested.
2) Use @Qualifier on the parameter to pick a bean by its bean name; the default bean name is the class name with a lowercase first letter.
3) When the multiple implementations are all needed and the choice is made at runtime (for example per country), inject them as List<PaymentGateway> or Map<String, PaymentGateway> keyed by bean name.

public OrderService(@Qualifier("razorpayGateway") PaymentGateway gateway) {
  this.gateway = gateway;
}

@Qualifier takes precedence over @Primary when both are present.

Because you cannot add @Component to library source code, declare the bean in a @Configuration class with a @Bean method. Spring calls the method once (singleton scope by default) and registers the returned object, named after the method name.

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
  @Bean
  public ModelMapper modelMapper() {
    return new ModelMapper();
  }
}

Services then receive it through constructor injection. @Component is a class-level annotation on your own classes; component scanning discovers the class and Spring creates the instance itself. @Bean is a method-level annotation that gives you full control over how the object is constructed and configured, so it is the usual choice for third-party classes or objects that need custom setup, such as a PasswordEncoder or RestClient.

The failure is a NoSuchBeanDefinitionException: no bean of type UserService exists in the application context, so Spring cannot inject it into UserController. The class is inside com.app, so component scanning does reach its package; the problem is that it is a plain class with no stereotype annotation, so scanning ignores it. Fix: annotate it with @Service (or register it with a @Bean method).

@Service
public class UserService {
  // business methods
}

Other causes of the same message: the class sits outside the main application package and is never scanned, UserService is an interface with no implementation bean, or the bean is only defined under a @Profile that is not active.

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {
  private final ProductService service;

  public ProductController(ProductService service) {
    this.service = service;
  }

  @GetMapping("/{id}")
  public ResponseEntity<ProductDto> getById(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return service.findById(id)
        .map(ResponseEntity::ok)
        .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
  }

  @PostMapping
  public ResponseEntity<ProductDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody ProductDto dto) {
    ProductDto saved = service.create(dto);
    URI location = ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequest()
        .path("/{id}").buildAndExpand(saved.id()).toUri();
    return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(saved);
  }
}

ResponseEntity lets each method control the status code, headers and body: ResponseEntity.ok for 200, notFound() for 404 and ResponseEntity.created for 201 with the Location header. The service is supplied by constructor injection and holds the business logic, @RequestBody with @Valid binds and validates the JSON input, and Jackson serializes the DTO to JSON.

// RegisterRequest.java
import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;

public record RegisterRequest(
  @NotBlank(message = "Name is required") String name,
  @NotBlank @Email(message = "Enter a valid email") String email,
  @NotBlank @Size(min = 8, message = "Password needs at least 8 characters") String password
) {}

// In the controller (import jakarta.validation.Valid)
@PostMapping("/register")
public ResponseEntity<UserResponse> register(@Valid @RequestBody RegisterRequest req) {
  return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(userService.register(req));
}

The constraints come from jakarta.validation (provided by spring-boot-starter-validation). @Valid on the @RequestBody parameter makes Spring validate the object before the method body runs; if a rule fails, Spring throws MethodArgumentNotValidException and responds with 400 Bad Request, which a @RestControllerAdvice can turn into a clean list of field errors. @Email and @Size treat null as valid, so they are combined with @NotBlank.

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

  @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
  public ResponseEntity<ApiError> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
        .body(new ApiError(404, ex.getMessage(), Instant.now()));
  }

  @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
  @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
  public Map<String, String> handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
    Map<String, String> errors = new HashMap<>();
    ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()
        .forEach(fe -> errors.put(fe.getField(), fe.getDefaultMessage()));
    return errors;
  }
}

public record ApiError(int status, String message, Instant timestamp) {}

@RestControllerAdvice applies these handlers to every controller and writes the return values as JSON, which gives centralized error handling: controllers and services just throw exceptions and never build error responses themselves. Each @ExceptionHandler handles one exception type; the status is set either through ResponseEntity or with @ResponseStatus. MethodArgumentNotValidException is thrown when @Valid fails on a @RequestBody. Spring 6 can also return ProblemDetail for the standard problem-details format, and a final @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class) returning 500 with a generic message avoids leaking stack traces.

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "students")
public class Student {
  @Id
  @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private Long id;

  @Column(nullable = false, length = 100)
  private String name;

  @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
  private String email;

  protected Student() {} // no-argument constructor required by JPA

  public Student(String name, String email) {
    this.name = name;
    this.email = email;
  }
  // getters and setters
}

@Entity maps the class to a table and @Id marks the primary key. GenerationType.IDENTITY uses MySQL AUTO_INCREMENT. The @Column attributes nullable and unique describe NOT NULL and UNIQUE constraints; Hibernate applies them when it generates the schema, while with Flyway you write them in the migration SQL. JPA needs a public or protected no-argument constructor, and Spring Boot 3 uses jakarta.persistence, not javax.persistence.

@Entity
public class Department {
  @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private Long id;
  private String name;

  @OneToMany(mappedBy = "department")
  private List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();

  public void addEmployee(Employee e) {
    employees.add(e);
    e.setDepartment(this);
  }
}

@Entity
public class Employee {
  @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private Long id;
  private String name;

  @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
  @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
  private Department department;
  // getters and setters
}

Employee (the @ManyToOne side) is the owning side because its table holds the department_id foreign key, and @JoinColumn names that column. mappedBy on Department marks the inverse side: JPA reads the relationship from Employee.department, does not create a join table and ignores changes made only to the list. The helper method addEmployee keeps both sides in sync in memory. @ManyToOne is EAGER by default, so it is set to FetchType.LAZY; @OneToMany is already lazy. Cascade options should be added only when the child's lifecycle depends on the parent, and REST responses should use DTOs to avoid infinite recursion.

question:
The project sets spring.jpa.open-in-view=false. This method throws LazyInitializationException: failed to lazily initialize a collection of role Department.employees, could not initialize proxy - no Session. Why, and how do you fix it?

@Service
public class DepartmentService {
  public DepartmentDto get(Long id) {
    Department d = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    return new DepartmentDto(d.getName(), d.getEmployees().size());
  }
}

This method should be all-or-nothing, but when the invoice upload fails the order row is still committed. Why?

@Transactional
public void placeOrder(OrderRequest req) throws IOException {
  orderRepository.save(toEntity(req));
  storageClient.uploadInvoice(req); // throws IOException
}

model_answer:
IOException is a checked exception, and Spring's default rollback rules for @Transactional roll back only on unchecked exceptions (RuntimeException and its subclasses) and Error. For a checked exception the transaction proxy commits the transaction and then rethrows the exception, so the saved order stays in the database.
Fixes:

@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void placeOrder(OrderRequest req) throws IOException { ... }

or use rollbackFor = IOException.class, or catch the IOException and rethrow it wrapped in a custom runtime exception such as StorageException. Also make sure nothing inside the method catches and logs the exception without rethrowing: a swallowed exception never reaches the proxy, so the transaction commits as well. Design note: calling a slow external service inside a database transaction keeps the transaction and its locks open; it is often better to upload before saving or after the commit.

question:
In this service, importAll() calls saveBatch() for every batch, but a failure in the middle of a batch leaves the earlier rows of that batch saved, as if there were no transaction. Why is @Transactional ignored, and how would you fix it?

@Service
public class ImportService {
  public void importAll(List<List<Row>> batches) {
    for (List<Row> batch : batches) {
      saveBatch(batch);
    }
  }

  @Transactional
  private void saveBatch(List<Row> rows) {
    rows.forEach(r -> repo.save(toEntity(r)));
  }
}

model_answer:
Spring applies @Transactional through a proxy that wraps the bean, so the annotation only takes effect when a call enters the bean through that proxy from outside. Two things break it here. First, saveBatch is a private method, and Spring's proxies cannot intercept private methods, so the annotation is silently ignored. Second, importAll calls saveBatch on this, which is self-invocation: the call never passes through the proxy, so even a public method would get no transaction. As a result each repo.save() runs in its own short transaction (Spring Data repository methods are transactional by default) and commits immediately, so earlier rows stay saved when a later row fails.
Fix: move the method into a separate bean and make it a public method, so each call goes through the proxy and every batch gets its own transaction:

@Service
public class BatchWriter {
  @Transactional
  public void saveBatch(List<Row> rows) { ... }
}

ImportService then injects BatchWriter and calls batchWriter.saveBatch(batch). Alternatives are programmatic transactions with TransactionTemplate inside the loop, self-injection of the proxy, or AspectJ weaving, but a separate bean is the cleanest design.

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

  @Bean
  public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
      .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // stateless API, no session cookies
      .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
      .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
        .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/products/**").permitAll()
        .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().authenticated())
      .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());
    return http.build();
  }

  @Bean
  public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
  }
}

In Spring Security 6 you expose a SecurityFilterChain bean built from HttpSecurity instead of extending the removed WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter, and use the lambda DSL with authorizeHttpRequests and requestMatchers. Rules are evaluated in order, so specific matchers come before anyRequest(). hasRole("ADMIN") checks the authority ROLE_ADMIN. Users are loaded by a UserDetailsService bean (for example one reading the users table), and their stored passwords must be BCrypt hashes. CSRF is disabled only because this API is stateless and does not rely on session cookies.

Enable method security with @EnableMethodSecurity on a @Configuration class; it replaces the deprecated @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity and turns on @PreAuthorize by default. Then annotate the method with a SpEL expression that is checked before it runs:

@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN') or #userId == authentication.principal.id")
public AccountDto getAccount(Long userId) { ... }

This assumes a custom UserDetails principal that exposes getId(). If the check fails, Spring throws AccessDeniedException, which is returned as 403 Forbidden; @PostAuthorize can check the returned object instead. hasRole('ADMIN') automatically adds the ROLE_ prefix and checks the authority ROLE_ADMIN, while hasAuthority matches the exact authority string, such as 'ROLE_ADMIN' or a fine-grained permission like 'orders:write'. Method security also works through proxies, so a self-invoked call bypasses it, just like @Transactional.

NULL means an unknown value, and SQL uses three-valued logic (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN). Any comparison with NULL using =, <> or != gives UNKNOWN, not TRUE, and WHERE keeps only rows where the condition is TRUE, so manager_id = NULL never matches. Use the IS NULL operator (IS NOT NULL for the opposite):

SELECT name FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL;

MySQL also has the NULL-safe equality operator <=>, so manager_id <=> NULL works too, and COALESCE(manager_id, 0) or IFNULL can replace NULL with a default when displaying results.

INSERT INTO customers (name, city) VALUES ('Asha Rao', 'Pune');

SELECT customer_id, name, city FROM customers WHERE city = 'Pune';

UPDATE customers SET city = 'Mumbai' WHERE customer_id = 7;

DELETE FROM customers WHERE customer_id = 12;

CRUD maps to INSERT (Create), SELECT (Read), UPDATE and DELETE. customer_id is AUTO_INCREMENT, so it is left out of the INSERT column list. UPDATE and DELETE must always have a WHERE clause, ideally on the primary key; without one every row is changed. The DELETE fails if orders still reference customer 12 through a foreign key, unless that key uses ON DELETE CASCADE.

question:
This query should show the highest-paid employee in each department, but MySQL 8.0 fails with error 1055 ('Expression #2 of SELECT list is not in GROUP BY clause and contains nonaggregated column ... incompatible with sql_mode=only_full_group_by'):
SELECT dept_id, name, MAX(salary) FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id;
Why does it fail, and how would you fix it correctly?

This query runs on every customer page load and takes about 3 seconds on an orders table with 5 million rows:
SELECT order_id, order_date, amount FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42 ORDER BY order_date DESC;
EXPLAIN shows type = ALL, key = NULL, rows around 5,000,000 and 'Using filesort'. What is the problem and how do you fix it? Table: orders(order_id, customer_id, order_date, amount).

model_answer:
type = ALL with key = NULL means a full table scan: MySQL reads all 5 million rows to find one customer's orders and then sorts them (Using filesort), because there is no usable index on customer_id. This is a missing index, common when the table was created without a foreign key on customer_id, since a foreign key would have created one. Add a composite index that matches both the filter and the sort:

CREATE INDEX idx_orders_cust_date ON orders (customer_id, order_date);

EXPLAIN should now show type = ref, key = idx_orders_cust_date, a small rows estimate and no filesort, because the rows for customer 42 are already ordered by order_date and are read backwards. Confirm the real timing with EXPLAIN ANALYZE (MySQL 8.0.18+). InnoDB builds a secondary index online, so reads and writes continue during the build.

By default MySQL runs with autocommit = 1, so every statement is committed immediately. START TRANSACTION (or BEGIN) opens an explicit transaction; its changes stay private until COMMIT makes them permanent, or ROLLBACK undoes them. SAVEPOINT marks a point you can partially roll back to.

START TRANSACTION;
INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, order_date, amount) VALUES (7, CURDATE(), 1500);
SAVEPOINT after_order;
UPDATE customers SET city = 'Pune' WHERE customer_id = 7;
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT after_order;  -- undo only the UPDATE
COMMIT;                             -- keep the INSERT

DDL statements such as CREATE or ALTER cause an implicit commit, and only a transactional engine such as InnoDB can roll back.

Run both updates in a single InnoDB transaction so they succeed or fail together (atomicity), and make the debit conditional:

START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 500
 WHERE account_id = 1 AND balance >= 500;
-- application: if affected rows = 0, ROLLBACK (insufficient funds)
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 500
 WHERE account_id = 2;
COMMIT;

If the server crashes before COMMIT, InnoDB crash recovery rolls the unfinished transaction back, so money is never debited without being credited; after COMMIT the change is durable. The balance >= 500 check happens inside the UPDATE while the row is locked, so two concurrent transfers cannot overdraw the account. A CHECK (balance >= 0) constraint adds a database-level safety net, and the application must ROLLBACK on any error.

// src/api/client.js
import axios from 'axios';
import { getToken, clearToken } from './tokenStore';

const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL, // e.g. http://localhost:8080/api
  timeout: 10000
});

api.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  const token = getToken();
  if (token) config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  return config;
});

api.interceptors.response.use(
  (response) => response,
  (error) => {
    const isLogin = error.config?.url?.includes('/auth/login');
    if (error.response?.status === 401 && !isLogin) {
      clearToken();
      window.location.assign('/login');
    }
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);

export default api;

axios.create builds one configured instance whose baseURL comes from the VITE_API_URL environment variable. The request interceptor adds the Authorization: Bearer header that Spring Security reads, and the response interceptor handles an expired or invalid token in one place (skipping the login call, so a wrong password still shows its error), then rejects the error so the calling component can still react. Components call api.get('/books') instead of repeating URLs and headers.

question:
A React form submits a new student, but the Spring Boot endpoint responds with 415 Unsupported Media Type. What is wrong?

await fetch('/api/students', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ name, email })
});

@PostMapping("/api/students")
public StudentDto create(@RequestBody StudentDto dto) { ... }

model_answer:
When fetch gets a string body and no headers, it sends Content-Type: text/plain;charset=UTF-8. @RequestBody asks Spring's HTTP message converters to turn the body into a StudentDto; the Jackson converter that can build a DTO only accepts application/json, so no converter matches, Spring throws HttpMediaTypeNotSupportedException and returns 415. Declare the JSON content type:

await fetch('/api/students', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name, email })
});

Axios sets this header automatically when you pass a plain object. Check the request headers and payload in the browser Network tab. Two related bugs give null fields instead of an error: a missing @RequestBody (Spring then binds from request parameters) and JSON keys that do not match the DTO property names (studentName vs name).

question:
A product is saved in MySQL (you can see the new row), but the React list does not show it until the page is refreshed. What is wrong with this code, and how do you fix it?

async function addProduct(form) {
  await api.post('/products', form);
  products.push(form);
  setProducts(products);
}

model_answer:
The code mutates the existing products array with push and passes the same reference to setProducts. React compares the old and new state with Object.is, sees the same array, and skips the re-render, so the row exists in MySQL but not on screen. It also adds the form object, which has no id: the database-generated id is only in the server response, so keys and later edit or delete calls would break. Use the response data and a functional update that creates a new array:

async function addProduct(form) {
  const { data: saved } = await api.post('/products', form);
  setProducts(prev => [...prev, saved]);
}

Alternatively, re-fetch the list after a successful POST (or invalidate the query when using a data-fetching library) so the UI always matches the database.

Use one consistent error format for the whole API, produced in one place on the backend and consumed in one place on the frontend.

Backend: a @RestControllerAdvice converts MethodArgumentNotValidException (thrown when @Valid fails) into 400 Bad Request with field errors:

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
  @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
  public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> onInvalid(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
    Map<String, String> fields = new HashMap<>();
    ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()
      .forEach(e -> fields.put(e.getField(), e.getDefaultMessage()));
    return ResponseEntity.badRequest()
      .body(Map.of("status", 400, "message", "Validation failed", "errors", fields));
  }
}

Response: {"status":400,"message":"Validation failed","errors":{"email":"must be a well-formed email address"}}. Spring's ProblemDetail format with an extra errors property is a standard alternative.

Frontend: the submit handler catches the error and, for a 400, stores the map in an error state object; each input renders errors[fieldName] below it:

try {
  await api.post('/students', form);
} catch (err) {
  if (err.response?.status === 400) setErrors(err.response.data.errors);
  else setFormError('Something went wrong, please try again');
}

The DTO field names must match the input names, and every endpoint uses the same shape, so one reusable form component can display the messages.

The Authorization header makes the browser send an OPTIONS preflight first, and a preflight never carries credentials. The Spring Security filter chain runs before the DispatcherServlet, so it rejects the unauthenticated OPTIONS request with 401 before the controller-level @CrossOrigin is ever applied, and that response has no CORS headers. Enable CORS in the security layer with one global configuration:

@Bean
SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
  http.cors(Customizer.withDefaults())
      .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // stateless JWT API
      .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
          .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
          .anyRequest().authenticated());
  return http.build(); // JWT filter setup omitted
}

@Bean
CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
  CorsConfiguration c = new CorsConfiguration();
  c.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("http://localhost:5173"));
  c.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
  c.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type"));
  UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource s = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
  s.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", c);
  return s;
}

With http.cors() enabled, Spring Security's CorsFilter answers the preflight with the right headers before authorization runs (without a CorsConfigurationSource bean it falls back to Spring MVC's CORS settings, including @CrossOrigin). Read the allowed origins from configuration per environment, and never combine allowCredentials(true) with a "*" origin (Spring rejects it; use allowedOriginPatterns if you need patterns).

question:
This login check works, but a security review flags it. What is the problem, and how do you fix it?

String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + email
    + "' AND password = '" + password + "'";
List<User> users = jdbcTemplate.query(sql, userRowMapper);

model_answer:
The query is built by string concatenation with user input, so it is vulnerable to SQL injection. Entering ' OR '1'='1 as the password turns the WHERE clause into (email = 'x' AND password = '') OR '1'='1', which is true for every row and logs the attacker in; other inputs could read or delete data. Use a parameterized query, where the SQL and the values are sent separately (JdbcTemplate uses a PreparedStatement with ? placeholders), so input is never parsed as SQL:

String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?";
List<User> users = jdbcTemplate.query(sql, userRowMapper, email);
if (users.isEmpty()
    || !passwordEncoder.matches(password, users.get(0).getPasswordHash())) {
  throw new BadCredentialsException("Invalid email or password");
}

Spring Data JPA derived methods such as findByEmail and @Query with bound parameters are also parameterized. The original code has a second flaw: it compares plain-text passwords in SQL, whereas passwords must be stored as BCrypt hashes and checked with matches(). React validation does not stop injection, and the database account should follow least privilege.

Layout: DNS points app.example.com to the server. Nginx listens on 443 (and redirects 80 to 443); Spring Boot listens only on 127.0.0.1:8080; MySQL listens on 3306 bound to localhost or a private network; the firewall opens only ports 80 and 443.

server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  server_name app.example.com;
  ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/privkey.pem;
  root /var/www/app/dist;

  location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;   # SPA fallback for React Router
  }
  location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
  }
}

What the reverse proxy solves: one public entry point; TLS termination in one place (for example Let's Encrypt certificates), so Spring Boot can use plain HTTP internally; the UI and API share the same origin, so no CORS configuration is needed in production; efficient static file serving with compression and cache headers; the backend and database stay hidden from the internet; and later load balancing across several Spring Boot instances with an upstream block. Set server.forward-headers-strategy=native (or framework) so Spring Boot sees the original HTTPS scheme and client IP.

The app uses client-side routing with React Router's BrowserRouter. Clicking links changes the URL through the History API without contacting the server, so navigation works. On a refresh or a direct link, the browser requests /dashboard from the server, but the build contains only index.html and static files, so Nginx finds no such file and returns 404. The Vite dev server hides this because it falls back to index.html automatically. Fix: configure the server to return index.html for any path that is not a real file, and let React Router render the route:

location / {
  try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
}

Keep /api requests in their own location so unknown API URLs still get real 404 responses from Spring Boot. If Spring Boot serves the React build itself, forward non-API routes to index.html in a controller; static hosting platforms offer equivalent rewrite rules. HashRouter also avoids the problem, but produces less clean URLs.
